By the end of this course, students will be able to:
- describe the main features of contemporary Polish society, culture, and art, and locate Poland accurately within Central Europe, distinguishing fact from the stereotypes commonly attached to the region;
- explain how key historical processes — the partitions, statelessness, war, communism, and 1989 — shaped present-day Polish identity;
- analyze Polish films, music, food, folklore, and literature as clues that reveal how Poland sees itself
- evaluate Poland's role in European and global contexts, including its economic, technological, and cultural ties to Taiwan and East Asia;
- reflect on the interplay of history, tradition, and modernity in shaping identity, and transfer that frame to their own cultural context through an original design project.

The course is adapted to students - beginners with no prior knowledge of the Czech language.
The main goal is to reach the A1/1 level at the end of this course. It is designed to:
- develop students' practical knowledge of the Czech language;
- enable students to communicate in situations where they find themselves as tourists (basic self-presentation, orientation in the city, in a restaurant, talking about family, activities, etc.);
- create strong self-confidence in speaking Czech;
- eliminate hesitation and fear of mistakes during productive language activities;
- be able to apply theoretical knowledge in practice
- arouse interest in Czech culture and related activities
Students should reach the A1/1 level according to the CEFRL in one semester (in addition to teaching Czech I), which corresponds to at least 3 lessons from the main teaching material Basics of Czech I (Adamovičová, A., Ivanovová, D.)

