Trong suốt khóa học, sinh viên được tiếp xúc với một thế giới đa dạng các ý tưởng và quan điểm về văn hóa, lịch sử và xã hội Việt Nam. Một số ý tưởng và quan điểm có thể mới lạ, thú vị, nhưng cũng có phần xa lạ với họ. Mục đích chính của khóa học này là cung cấp cho sinh viên những kiến ​​thức mới về văn hóa, lịch sử và xã hội Việt Nam, đôi khi vượt xa những gì họ đã biết, để sinh viên có thể đánh giá các cách hiểu của người khác với một tư duy cởi mở nhưng phê phán. Sau khóa học, sinh viên sẽ có thể hiểu được:
Những đặc điểm cơ bản về thiên nhiên và con người Việt Nam;
Các giá trị văn hóa truyền thống của Việt Nam trong lịch sử cũng như trong quá trình toàn cầu hóa hiện nay;
Xã hội Việt Nam xưa và nay.
Để đạt được điều này, phương pháp giảng dạy sẽ sử dụng sự kết hợp cân bằng giữa bài giảng, thảo luận và thuyết trình của sinh viên.

Course Objectives:
(1) To introduce students to the basic theoretical assumptions and tools in Generative Syntax and Usage-based Construction Grammar;
(2) To develop a basic understanding of linguistic research methodology, e.g. introspection-based grammaticality judgement and experimental and corpus-based approaches;
(3) To broaden students’ knowledge of a variety of theoretical traditions and enhance their ability to evaluate them critically.
Learning Outcomes:
(1) Academic skills:
(a) Ability to engage critically with previous studies;
(b) Ability to describe and analyse data.
(2) Transferable skills:
(a) Ability to evaluate and process complex information;
(b) Ability to produce complex information in an accessible way;
(c) Metalinguistic knowledge of English that is conducive to teaching English.

Course Goals
Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:
identify and analyze phonological structures and patterns in linguistic data;
conduct systematic qualitative analyses of phonological phenomena;
analyze the structural organization of speech sounds, syllables and sound sequences;
formulate clear and evidence-based phonological generalizations;
recognize, construct and critically evaluate phonological arguments;
compare phonological patterns across languages and assess their cross-linguistic significance;
relate empirical phonological data to relevant theoretical concepts and analytical frameworks;
evaluate alternative analyses and identify the assumptions underlying different interpretations;
explain how phonological structures and sound-sequence patterns can inform the representation and analysis of language data in artificial intelligence and computational systems;
apply phonological analysis to research areas such as language acquisition, second-language learning, speech and language disorders, corpus linguistics, speech technology and AI-assisted language research; and
develop an independent research project involving the analysis and interpretation of phonological data.

Course Goals
Upon completion of the course, students will be able to:
critically examine the interaction between phonological form, phonetic realization, frequency, and probability in Mandarin;
distinguish categorical phonological patterns from gradient phonetic and distributional patterns;
analyze Mandarin segments, syllables, tones, sound sequences, and prosodic structures using advanced phonological and phonetic frameworks;
evaluate how lexical frequency, segment frequency, phonotactic probability, contextual predictability, and usage experience may influence speech patterns;
formulate and assess competing structural, articulatory, perceptual, probabilistic, and usage-based explanations of linguistic data;
investigate the relationship between phonetic variation and phonological representation;
integrate qualitative linguistic analysis with corpus-based, experimental, acoustic, and quantitative evidence;
critically interpret statistical patterns without treating frequency or correlation as sufficient explanations of linguistic structure;
compare Mandarin phonological and phonetic patterns with relevant cross-linguistic evidence;
evaluate theoretical approaches to the phonology–phonetics interface, probabilistic phonology, exemplar-based representation, usage-based phonology, and distributional learning;
examine how phonological structures and sound-sequence probabilities can be represented in computational and artificial-intelligence systems;
assess the contributions and limitations of machine-learning and data-driven approaches to the analysis of speech and phonological structure;
apply advanced phonological and phonetic analysis to language acquisition, second-language speech, speech disorders, sociophonetics, corpus linguistics, and speech technology;
identify theoretically significant research questions from complex Mandarin speech data;
construct original, evidence-based arguments appropriate for doctoral-level research; and
design and complete an independent research project integrating phonological theory, phonetic evidence, and frequency- or probability-based analysis.
use both Python and R for data processing, statistical analysis, visualization, and the computational analysis of phonological and phonetic patterns; proficiency in both programming environments is required for this course. Instruction in agent-based modelling will be provided by Dr. Chainwu Lee, Consultant to the Phonetics and Psycholinguistics Laboratory. When required by the research project, students may also need to participate in participant recruitment and data collection at collaborating medical institutions.

The primary objectives of this course include:
Reexamine and reevaluate the major theoretical ap.roaches to the study of international relations, namely, realism, liberalism and social constructivism;
An in-depth study of the origins and the ending of the 40-year long Cold War;
Two case studies, including the Cuban Missile Crisis of 1962 and The Democratic Peace theory.
Through these studies, we hope to develop analytical and critical capabilities on international affairs and on the strategic, security and foreign polices of major actors in world affairs.

